      "q": "3.\nDefine ledger accounts and draw cash ledger format.",
      "a": "The ledger is a book of accounts in which specific transactions are documented. The ledger holds all credit, debit, account, and journal information that will come in handy later. Each account in a ledger account is kept on its own page or combined.\nLedgers are important documents for accounting purposes, as it helps to find out how many types of transactions are made in single. For the purpose of easy posting and location accounts are generally opened in ledger. For big organizations the ledger account register contains indexes which make it much easier to find out the details quickly. Following is the format for ledger account of Cash:\nCash Account\nDate\nParticular\nJ.\nF.\nAmount(Dr.)\nDate\nParticular\nJ.\nF.\nAmount(Cr.)\nTo Capital\n-\nBy Bank\n-\nBy Purchase\n-\nBy Cartage\n-"
